VISUAL: Title card: "Fungal Robots Just Got an Upgrade. But Why Fungi?" with images of a robot, a fungal growth, and a person.
AUDIO: 'There\'s been progress in using fungi as robots. Do you remember when scientists gave fungi a body to operate and showed that they could perceive light and avoid it and could learn how to use that body over time? There was also the suggestion of using them as a fungal skin, which you can see with the Terminator bot up'

[00:13] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A research paper titled "Mycoelectronics: Bioprinted living fungal bioelectronics for artificial sensation" is displayed, showing images of fungal bioelectronics on skin and being handled.
AUDIO: 'there, which I think was delightful. That research did not simply end. And one of the reasons that you would want to use fungi as a form of artificial intelligence for a robot is because they already have many of the things that we want. They\'re capable of communicating'

[00:25] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A research paper titled "Mycoelectronics: Bioprinted living fungal bioelectronics for artificial sensation" is displayed, showing images of fungal bioelectronics on skin and being handled.
AUDIO: 'with each other. They do know at least 50 words, at least ones that we\'ve identified, although have not translated yet, and they can sense the environment and respond to it accordingly. So, why wouldn\'t you want to use them as a robot? They\'re also quite a bit sturdier'

[00:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A research paper titled "Mycoelectronics: Bioprinted living fungal bioelectronics for artificial sensation" is displayed, showing images of fungal bioelectronics on skin and being handled.
AUDIO: 'than some of our other biohybrid robotics, like, you know, brain organoids. Fungi already have to live in contact with air and changing environments. They can survive water loss. So, unlike biohybrids like brain'

[00:50] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A research paper titled "Mycoelectronics: Bioprinted living fungal bioelectronics for artificial sensation" is displayed, showing images of fungal bioelectronics on skin and being handled.
AUDIO: 'organoids, they\'re a lot easier to keep alive. I did find this paper slightly unsettling because it has the suggestion of using fungi as an interface for things like prosthetics, synthetic skin, and I just don\'t like that, and I don\'t have a good reason to not like it. It'

